_ebitda":false,"_score_net_debt_ebitda":0.0,"_gate_accruals":-0.18263788337501646,"_gp_accruals":true,"_score_accruals":88.0,"_gate_rev_volatility":0.13945864622625792,"_gp_rev_volatility":false,"_score_rev_volatility":74.4,"_gate_margin_vs_hist":-0.05834739778170508,"_gp_margin_vs_hist":true,"_score_margin_vs_hist":100.0,"_gate_piotroski":5,"_gp_piotroski":false,"_score_piotroski":55.6,"_gate_spread":-0.050669674905137935,"_gp_spread":false,"_score_spread":0.0,"_gate_roic_consistency":0.9683806413734428,"_gp_roic_consistency":false,"_score_roic_consistency":0.0,"_gate_incr_roic":-0.5678133097065126,"_gp_incr_roic":false,"_score_incr_roic":0.0,"_gate_margin_advantage":-0.07275771326288644,"_gp_margin_advantage":false,"_score_margin_advantage":9.1,"_gate_pool_share":-0.35460133440930086,"_gp_pool_share":false,"_score_pool_share":0.0,"_gate_rev_durability":0.012489747732411738,"_gp_rev_durability":false,"_score_rev_durability":31.2,"_gate_fcf_durability":0.07227071208607616,"_gp_fcf_durability":true,"_score_fcf_durability":61.1,"_gate_margins":-0.013180872991597856,"_gp_margins":false,"_score_margins":36.8,"_gate_fund_growth":0,"_gp_fund_growth":false,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.0,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":11.1,"_gate_share_shrink":0.04283511973265508,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.008200016078462899,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":86.3,"_gate_insider_own":0.23324,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":100.0,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Compass Minerals International, Inc. provides essential minerals in the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom, and internationally. It operates through two segments, Salt and Plant Nutrition. The Salt segment produces, markets, and sells sodium chloride and magnesium chloride, including rock salt, mechanically and solar evaporated salt, and brine and flake magnesium chloride products; and purchases and sells potassium chloride and calcium chloride to sell as finished products or to blend with sodium chloride to produce specialty products. This segment provides products for use as a deicer for roadways, consumer, and professional use; as an ingredient in chemical production; for water treatment, human, and animal nutrition; and for various other consumer and industrial uses, as well as records management services. The Plant Nutrition segment produces sulfate of potash specialty fertilizers in various grades that are used in broadcast spreaders, direct application, and liquid fertilizer solutions under the Protassium+ brand name; turf products used by the turf and ornamental markets, as well as for blends used on golf course greens; and organic products. This segment provides its products to distributors and retailers of crop inputs, as well as growers. The company was formerly known as Salt Holdings Corporation and changed its name to Compass Minerals International, Inc. in December 2003. Compass Minerals International, Inc. was founded in 1844 and is headquartered in Overland Park, Kansas.","ceo_bio":"Dr. Edward C. Dowling Jr., B.Sc., M.Sc., Ph.D. | President, CEO & Director | Age 70 | Compensation: $2.8M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 1K people. Revenue per employee of $691K ranks in the above the sector median among Basic Materials peers. Each employee generates $71K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $6K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (9/10).","financial_summary":["Compass Minerals Intl Inc is a small-cap basic materials business with a $946M market cap on $1.2B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -0% over the past three years and +1% annually across the full decade \u2014 essentially flat, and a business treading water is rarely a path to compounding.","Returns on capital run at 3% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, and 23% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $128M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 10 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 6.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 0.4\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 23% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and profit capture is only 0.2\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.5) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins softening -1.5pp YoY \u2014 early warning on the moat","sev":"amber","cat":"trend"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 5.5% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Value-trap profile (score 57/100): leveraged balance sheet under pressure; roic below cost of capital \u2014 cheapness may reflect deterioration, not mispricing","sev":"amber","cat":"value_trap"},{"text":"Carries more leverage than most Basic Materials peers \u2014 a smaller cushion for the inevitable bad year","sev":"amber","cat":"balance_sheet"},{"text":"Weakness in commodity prices pressures materials companies","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Trade policy and tariff whiplash \u2014 materials producers are first-order casualties of protectionist swings, and margin gets squeezed from both input and output pricing.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading at a 37% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Compass Minerals International, Inc. (NYSE:CMP) Stock Rated \u201cReduce\u201d by Wall Street Analysts","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/12/compass-minerals-international-inc-nysecmp-stock-rated-reduce-by-wall-street-analysts.html","date":"2026-11-12","timestamp":1794474194.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.511,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Behavioral Patterns of CMP and Institutional Flows","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/23/Behavioral_Patterns_of_CMP_and_Institutional_Flows_110626101201_1793977921.html","date":"2026-11-06","timestamp":1793977921.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"United States Antimony Corporation (UAMY) Reports Q3 Loss, Lags Revenue Estimates","source":"finance.yahoo.com","link":"https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/united-states-antimony-corporation-uamy-212503846.html","date":"2026-11-12","timestamp":1794518703.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.318,"tier":2,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"Triumph Gold Names Russell Ball as Board Chair and Interim CEO Amid Leadership Changes","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/ca/n